STEP 1 -- designed: baseline, the RMON MIB with the feature OFF               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: read the RMON subtree over SNMP before any rmon command is sent.  Read sysName in
  the same leg, so a silent agent cannot be mistaken for an absent MIB.

$ snmpq walk 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1 --v2c --community testing123 --timeout 5 --retries 1
  CMD  /usr/bin/snmpwalk -On -t 5.0 -r 1 -v 2c -c testing123 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1 = No Such Object available on this agent at this OID
  RESULT NOSUCH

$ snmpq walk 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.16 --v2c --community testing123 --timeout 5 --retries 1
  CMD  /usr/bin/snmpwalk -On -t 5.0 -r 1 -v 2c -c testing123 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.16
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.16 = No Such Object available on this agent at this OID
  RESULT NOSUCH                            <-- the whole RMON MIB is absent

$ snmpq get 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 --v2c --community testing123 --timeout 5 --retries 1
  CMD  /usr/bin/snmpget -On -t 5.0 -r 1 -v 2c -c testing123 10.38.22.34 1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0
  | .1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 = STRING: awplus
  RESULT VALUE                             <-- the control: the agent does answer

  On the CLI, all four of `show rmon alarm`, `show rmon event`, `show rmon statistics` and
  `show rmon history` printed nothing at all: no header, no empty table and no error.
  => STEP 1 PASS: the RMON MIB does not exist before it is configured.  The agent answered
     sysName in the same leg.  So the NOSUCH is a measurement, not a dead link.  Everything
     measured later is produced by the RMON configuration and by nothing else.

STEP 2 -- designed: read the RMON CLI surface before sending any command      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: tools/qmark.py types `PREFIX ?`, reads the help, then clears the line with Ctrl-U.
  It never sends a carriage return, so nothing is executed.  Its self-test printed
  `self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U then CR executed nothing`.

  Config mode:
    rmon alarm 1 ?            ->  WORD  variable for setting alarm(etherStatsEntry.n.n)
    rmon event 1 ?            ->  description | log | owner | trap | <cr>
    rmon event 1 trap ?       ->  WORD  event community
    snmp-server community ?   ->  WORD  Community name
    snmp-server community testing123 ?  ->  <1-99> | ro | rw | view | <cr>
    snmp-server host ?        ->  A.B.C.D | X:X::X:X          (NO port option at any level)
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 ?        -> WORD | informs | traps | version | vrf
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version ? -> 1 | 2c | 3

  The full alarm grammar, read token by token:
    rmon alarm 1 etherStatsEntry.5.1 ?                                  -> interval
    ... interval 10 ?                                                   -> absolute | delta
    ... interval 10 delta ?                                             -> rising-threshold
    ... delta rising-threshold ?                                        -> <1-2147483647>
    ... rising-threshold 10 ?                                           -> event
    ... event 1 ?                                                       -> falling-threshold
    ... falling-threshold ?                                             -> <1-2147483647>
    ... falling-threshold 1 event 2 ?                                   -> alarmstartup | owner | <cr>
    ... falling-threshold 1 event 12 alarmstartup ?                     -> <1-3>  Alarm StartUp value

  Interface mode, on port1.0.10:
    rmon collection ?           ->  history | stats
    rmon collection stats ?     ->  <1-65535>  Stat Index
    rmon collection stats 1 ?   ->  owner | <cr>
    rmon collection history ?   ->  <1-65535>  history Index
    rmon collection history 1 ? ->  buckets (default 50) | interval (default 1800) | owner | <cr>
    snmp trap ?                 ->  link-status | mac-change

  `snmp-server enable trap ?` offered 32 groups.  `rmon` is one of them.

  PRODUCT DEVIATION 1 of 2 -- an out-of-range number is reported against the NEXT keyword:

awplus(config)# rmon alarm 12 etherStatsEntry.6.11 interval 10 delta rising-threshold 1
                event 11 falling-threshold 0 event 12 alarmstartup ?
    % Unrecognized command

  The same line with `falling-threshold 1` in place of `falling-threshold 0` answered
  `<1-3>  Alarm StartUp value`.  So `alarmstartup` is a valid keyword.  The real error is
  the `0`, eleven tokens earlier.  Both thresholds accept `<1-2147483647>`.
  Two effects.  First, the error message points at the wrong word.  An engineer deletes
  `alarmstartup`, the line still fails, and the real cause is never suspected.  Second,
  `alarmFallingThreshold = 0` cannot be configured on this build.  RFC 2819 sets no such
  floor.  For a `delta` alarm on a packet counter, "the rate fell to zero" is the most
  natural falling condition.  Here the lowest value you can type is 1.  So "no traffic at
  all" and "one packet" cannot be told apart.
  => STEP 2 PASS: every CLI token this case later sent was read off the device first, with
     no command executed.  One deviation in the CLI was found and is recorded above.

